{
  "summary": "The app largely follows the DESIGN.md specifications and passes automated accessibility (axe 0 issues, Lighthouse accessibility 98) and style checks (all CSS classes matched). All screens exist and the markup is semantic. However, the primary end‑to‑end flow breaks on the backup feature – the expected \"Backup ready to share\" Snackbar never appears, causing the UI‑flow test to fail on both phone and desktop. This is a high‑severity regression because the core backup‑restore path is incomplete. Performance is modest (Lighthouse 57) and could be improved. Minor issues around focus‑visible styling and token usage were also observed.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "high",
      "file": "src/screens/BackupPassphraseScreen.tsx",
      "issue": "Backup success state does not render the expected \"Backup ready to share\" Snackbar (or navigate to the success screen), causing the primary flow test to time‑out.",
      "fix": "After the backup generation completes, trigger the share sheet and display a Snackbar with the exact copy \"Backup ready to share.\" Ensure the Snackbar is mounted in the component tree and that its visibility is awaited in tests. If a separate success screen is used, update the test to expect the screen’s copy or, better, keep the Snackbar as spec‑defined."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "file": "src/App.tsx (bundle entry)",
      "issue": "Lighthouse performance score is only 57, indicating heavy JS bundle size and slow initial load.",
      "fix": "Enable Hermes (if not already), lazy‑load screens with React Navigation’s `lazy` option, split off heavy libraries (e.g., PDF generation) into separate bundles, and remove unused imports. Consider using `expo-optimize` for assets and ensure `expo-splash-screen` is configured to hide only after initial data load."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "src/components/ui/PrimaryButton.tsx",
      "issue": "Focus‑visible outline (2 dp outline using `theme.colors.outline`) is not applied on web, reducing keyboard navigation clarity.",
      "fix": "Add a `:focus-visible` style (or `onFocus`/`onBlur` handling) that applies a 2 dp border of `theme.colors.outline`. Verify the style works across browsers and native platforms."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "src/components/ui/EmptyState.tsx",
      "issue": "The placeholder illustration lacks an `accessibilityLabel`/`aria-label` for screen readers.",
      "fix": "Add `accessibilityLabel=\"Empty state illustration\"` (and `accessible={true}` on the wrapper) so assistive technologies can announce the illustration purpose."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "src/components/ui/SettingSwitch.tsx",
      "issue": "Switch on/off colors are hard‑coded instead of using the secondary token (`#015958` for on thumb, `#FFFFFF` for on track).",
      "fix": "Replace inline color values with `theme.colors.secondary` and `theme.colors.onSecondary` for the thumb, and `theme.colors.surface` / `theme.colors.onSurface` for the track, ensuring contrast compliance."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "score": 7,
  "summary": "The policy gate screen is clean and functional, with clear visual hierarchy and a single primary action. Typography is readable and consistent, spacing is generous, and alignment is centered. However, it lacks polish in form design (checkbox label placement, button styling) and visual hierarchy (no clear focus on the checkbox), and the desktop version is underutilized. It meets the core requirements but feels like a first draft of a polished product.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "viewport": "phone",
      "issue": "The checkbox and its label are misaligned; the label is positioned below the checkbox with no clear visual connection, making it hard to associate with the checkbox.",
      "fix": "Place the checkbox and label on the same line with appropriate padding, or use a checkbox with inline label for better visual grouping."
    },
    {
      "severity": "medium",
      "viewport": "phone",
      "issue": "The 'Accept' button is centered but lacks visual emphasis or feedback when enabled/disabled, making it less discoverable.",
      "fix": "Add a subtle state change (e.g., border, background color) when enabled, and ensure the button is visually distinct from the surrounding text."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "viewport": "phone",
      "issue": "The 'Tick the box above to continue' hint is centered and small, making it less prominent than the button.",
      "fix": "Move the hint to the bottom of the screen with a smaller font size, or use a subtle underline or icon to indicate the required action."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "viewport": "desktop",
      "issue": "The desktop layout is identical to the phone layout, with no adaptation for the larger screen (e.g., no horizontal spacing, no reflow, no use of available space).",
      "fix": "Add horizontal padding or spacing to utilize the desktop width, or break the policy text into columns or sections for better readability on larger screens."
    },
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "viewport": "both",
      "issue": "The policy text is presented as a single block without clear visual hierarchy or sectioning, making it harder to scan.",
      "fix": "Use subtle visual cues (e.g., bold headings, indentation, or subtle dividers) to separate the three policy sections for better readability."
    }
  ]
}

{
